chapter(3,'evs','taking-charge-of-waste','Taking Charge of Waste',
 ("National Cleanliness Day","Children and their teacher are cleaning a park on National Cleanliness Day. Why? Have you seen piles of waste near your home or school? Today we find out how waste is made and how we can take charge of it."),
 [("How is waste created?",["We make a lot of waste in our everyday activities.","We throw away old clothes, tins, bottles, paper, vegetable and fruit peels, plastic wrappers, pencil shavings, old batteries and much more.","Packaged products, take-away food, plastic and decorations all create waste.","We try to throw waste away, but it often ends up in garbage piles near our homes, schools and neighbourhoods.","How do we feel about this, and can we do something about it? Managing waste is in our own hands."]),
  ("Why is garbage a problem?",["Trash lying on the roadside can harm animals, because cows and other animals can die by eating plastic mixed with food.","Some people burn plastic, which gives out harmful gases.","People fall ill because of the garbage around them.","Puddles and wastewater flowing through roads bring mosquitoes whose bites can cause diseases, and the wastewater may carry germs.","But in some places people have worked hard to clean their houses and surroundings, and we can learn from them."]),
  ("The first rule: Reduce",["The first rule is to REDUCE the amount of waste we make.","Many people avoid food and drinks packed in wrappers or plastic bottles, and carry cloth bags instead of plastic bags.","The more things we buy, the more we throw away, so we should buy only what we need and use it until it is no longer usable.","We can share old toys and books with children who need them and cannot afford to buy them."]),
  ("The second rule: Reuse",["The second rule is to REUSE things instead of throwing them away.","Our grandparents made quilts and bags from old sarees and clothes, and gave old clothes to those in need.","We can wrap gifts in newspaper or old cloth, which saves resources and adds a personal touch.","We can use paper used on one side for rough work, refill a steel or copper bottle instead of buying new ones, and make toys and decorations from old newspapers, calendars, bottles and boxes.","We can even make a paper bag from newspaper with no scissors and no glue, and give a thank-you gift made from waste to the person who keeps our locality clean."]),
  ("A place of pride",["Silluk, a village in Arunachal Pradesh, has clean roads and tree-lined streets that feel like a garden.","It won an award and is called a zero waste village because it manages its waste so well.","Chhota Narena was one of the first villages to become waste-free.","Cities like Indore and Mysuru are known for being clean.","We can ask our elders about a clean village or town they know, and find out about the Swachh Bharat Mission."]),
  ("Separate the waste: green and blue dustbins",["We often see dustbins of two colours at schools, markets, parks, bus stands, railway platforms and melas.","The green dustbin is for things that rot easily and mix with soil, like dried leaves, fruit peels, vegetable waste and egg shells.","Waste in the green bin can be turned into compost, which is food for plants and for tiny animals in the soil.","The blue dustbin is for things made of metal, glass, plastic and paper, which can be made into new objects. This is called recycling.","Some villages keep separate bins for each material so it is easier to send them for recycling.","Sharp things like broken glass or needles should be wrapped in cloth and paper so sanitation workers are not hurt."]),
  ("Keeping our surroundings clean",["Each of us can help keep our home, school, neighbourhood, city and country clean.","We can keep our room tidy and learn the names of cleaning tools like brooms, mops and dustpans.","On National Cleanliness Day, 30 January, we can take a pledge to keep ourselves and our surroundings clean.","Our aim is Want not, Waste not."])],
 [("Which dustbin do these go in: onion peels, plastic bottle, eggshell, paper, light bulb, rotten fruit?",[("Onion peels rot easily, so they go in the green dustbin.","Green is for things that rot and make compost."),("Eggshells and rotten fruit also go in the green dustbin.","They are natural waste that mixes with soil."),("Plastic bottle and paper go in the blue dustbin.","They can be recycled."),("A light bulb is glass, so it goes in the blue dustbin, wrapped carefully if broken.","Glass can be recycled and broken glass can hurt workers.")]),
  ("Plan a zero-waste birthday party.",[("Use cloth or reusable plates and cups instead of plastic ones.","This reduces waste."),("Wrap gifts in newspaper or old cloth.","This reuses material."),("Share extra food and put peels in the green dustbin.","Sharing and compost reduce waste.")]),
  ("List three ways to reduce waste at home.",[("Carry a cloth bag to the shop.","It reduces plastic bags."),("Refill a steel bottle instead of buying new ones.","It reduces plastic bottles."),("Use paper on both sides and share old toys and books.","Reusing and sharing reduce waste.")])],
 ("Tell a friend what reduce, reuse and the two dustbins mean.",["Reduce means making less waste by buying only what we need","Reuse means using things again instead of throwing them away","Green dustbin is for things that rot and become compost","Blue dustbin is for metal, glass, plastic and paper that can be recycled"],["Waste is not our problem","All waste goes in one dustbin","Burning plastic is a good way to get rid of it"]),
 ["Waste is made by our everyday activities and we can manage it ourselves.","Garbage and wastewater cause illness, mosquitoes and harm to animals.","Reduce and reuse are the first two rules.","The green dustbin is for waste that rots into compost; the blue one is for materials that can be recycled.","Villages like Silluk show that a clean place is possible; our aim is Want not, Waste not."],
 [M("tw-1",1,"What does REDUCE mean?","Make less waste by buying only what we need",[("Throw more things away","That makes more waste."),("Burn the waste","Burning plastic gives harmful gases."),("Leave waste on the road","That harms animals and people.")],"Reduce is the first rule of managing waste.",None),
  M("tw-2",1,"Which dustbin is for fruit peels and dry leaves?","Green",[("Blue","Blue is for recyclable materials."),("Red","The textbook uses green and blue."),("Black","The textbook uses green and blue.")],"Green is for waste that rots and becomes compost.",None),
  F("tw-3",1,"Plastic, glass, metal and paper go in the blue dustbin.",True,"These can be recycled into new objects."),
  M("tw-4",1,"What is Silluk known as?","A zero waste village in Arunachal Pradesh",[("A big city in Gujarat","Silluk is a village."),("A kind of dustbin","It is a village."),("A cleaning tool","It is a village that manages waste well.")],"Silluk won an award for waste management.",None),
  S("tw-5",2,"Using things again instead of throwing them away is called ____.",["reuse","Reuse"],"Reuse is the second rule.",[("reduce","Reduce means making less waste.")]),
  M("tw-6",2,"Why is burning plastic harmful?","It releases harmful gases",[("It makes plastic cleaner","It pollutes the air."),("It gives free energy to everyone","It harms health."),("It makes the sky blue","That is not true.")],"Burning plastic pollutes the air and harms health.",None),
  M("tw-7",2,"What do puddles and wastewater bring?","Mosquitoes and germs that cause diseases",[("Fresh air","They bring mosquitoes."),("Tasty food","They carry germs."),("Cool shade","They spread disease.")],"Mosquitoes breed in dirty water and spread diseases.",None),
  F("tw-8",2,"Green dustbin waste can be turned into compost.",True,"Compost is food for plants and soil animals."),
  M("tw-9",2,"A cloth bag instead of a plastic bag helps because it ____.","Reduces plastic waste",[("Makes more waste","It reduces waste."),("Cannot be used again","A cloth bag can be reused many times."),("Is more expensive in every case","Reduce means less waste.")],"Cloth bags can be used again and again.",None),
  M("tw-10",3,"Why wrap broken glass in cloth and paper before putting it in the bin?","So sanitation workers are not hurt",[("To make it look nice","The reason is safety."),("To hide it","The reason is safety."),("To make it heavier","The reason is safety.")],"Sharp waste can injure the people who collect it.",None),
  S("tw-11",3,"Making new objects from thrown-away metal, glass, plastic or paper is called ____.",["recycling","recycle"],"Recycling turns old materials into new objects.",[("composting","Composting is for waste that rots.")]),
  M("tw-12",3,"Which is the best way to give gifts with less waste?","Wrap in old cloth or newspaper",[("Use lots of plastic wrap","That creates more waste."),("Throw the wrapper at the road","That is litter."),("Burn the paper","That is harmful.")],"Reusing cloth or newspaper reduces waste.",None)],
 svgs=[("c3-waste-bins","The green and blue dustbins with the waste each one takes","Green is for waste that rots and becomes compost; blue is for waste that can be recycled.")])
